1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,843
Imagine you find a recording
from nineteen fifty.

2
00:00:04,180 --> 00:00:07,490
It is exactly what you need for
the documentary you are making.

3
00:00:08,285 --> 00:00:10,575
Nobody sells it any more.

4
00:00:10,725 --> 00:00:13,475
The company that released
it vanished decades ago.

5
00:00:14,125 --> 00:00:15,935
The musician died long ago.

6
00:00:16,662 --> 00:00:18,442
You want ten seconds of it.

7
00:00:18,522 --> 00:00:20,952
And you may not be allowed to have them.

8
00:00:21,688 --> 00:00:22,888
Not because it is secret.

9
00:00:22,968 --> 00:00:26,258
Not because anyone else is using it.

10
00:00:26,488 --> 00:00:28,778
But because somebody may still own it.

11
00:00:29,468 --> 00:00:31,038
And nobody knows who.

12
00:00:32,247 --> 00:00:33,777
So here is the question.

13
00:00:34,567 --> 00:00:37,637
Can copyright get in the way of the
thing it was built to encourage?

14
00:00:40,407 --> 00:00:42,377
Let's start somewhere important.

15
00:00:43,167 --> 00:00:45,137
Copyright is not a stupid idea.

16
00:00:46,030 --> 00:00:49,600
Imagine you spend three years
writing a book, and publish it.

17
00:00:50,370 --> 00:00:54,540
The next morning somebody prints a
million copies and undercuts you by half.

18
00:00:55,365 --> 00:00:57,175
You did the work. They did not.

19
00:00:58,107 --> 00:00:59,517
So the bargain is this.

20
00:01:00,647 --> 00:01:02,377
Society gives you a temporary monopoly.

21
00:01:03,287 --> 00:01:05,237
You get your chance to earn from it.

22
00:01:05,807 --> 00:01:07,797
And eventually, it belongs to everybody.

23
00:01:08,747 --> 00:01:10,917
That last part is the whole point.

24
00:01:11,567 --> 00:01:13,837
It was never the creator
owns this forever.

25
00:01:15,155 --> 00:01:16,845
So how long is temporary?

26
00:01:18,168 --> 00:01:21,088
The Berne Convention, which
most countries have signed,

27
00:01:21,168 --> 00:01:24,358
sets the floor at the life of
the author plus fifty years.

28
00:01:25,197 --> 00:01:29,507
Britain, the European Union and the
United States all went further than that.

29
00:01:30,197 --> 00:01:32,767
The life of the author,
plus seventy years.

30
00:01:33,594 --> 00:01:35,044
Work out what that means.

31
00:01:35,774 --> 00:01:37,924
Someone writes a song at twenty-five.

32
00:01:38,274 --> 00:01:39,227
They live to eighty-five.

33
00:01:39,307 --> 00:01:42,904
Their song is locked up for
a hundred and thirty years.

34
00:01:44,321 --> 00:01:46,681
Steamboat Willie, the
first Mickey Mouse cartoon

35
00:01:46,761 --> 00:01:50,311
ever released, came out
in nineteen twenty-eight.

36
00:01:50,621 --> 00:01:53,211
It became free to use on the first of
January, two thousand and twenty-four.

37
00:01:56,487 --> 00:01:57,687
Ninety-five years.

38
00:02:00,027 --> 00:02:03,997
And that number matters, because of
the way progress actually happens.

39
00:02:04,901 --> 00:02:06,591
Nobody starts from nothing.

40
00:02:07,161 --> 00:02:08,641
Someone discovers something.

41
00:02:08,721 --> 00:02:09,941
Someone else studies it.

42
00:02:10,021 --> 00:02:11,371
Someone improves it.

43
00:02:11,601 --> 00:02:13,511
And someone builds on that.

44
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:16,550
Which leaves us with a tension.

45
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:19,430
Copyright says we want
to encourage creation.

46
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:23,970
But creating usually means: let me
use something that already exists.

47
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:29,930
Music shows this best, because
a song is not one thing.

48
00:02:30,707 --> 00:02:35,437
There is the composition. The lyrics.
The arrangement. The recording itself.

49
00:02:36,147 --> 00:02:38,677
The performance. And anything
sampled from elsewhere.

50
00:02:39,617 --> 00:02:42,327
Every one of those can
belong to somebody different.

51
00:02:43,169 --> 00:02:47,579
So you take two seconds of an
old recording. You slow it down.

52
00:02:48,309 --> 00:02:51,099
You cut it into pieces. You
bury it under a new beat.

53
00:02:51,973 --> 00:02:53,763
The original is barely audible.

54
00:02:54,313 --> 00:02:55,703
You have made something new.

55
00:02:56,393 --> 00:02:58,003
Legally, it may still count.

56
00:02:58,879 --> 00:03:00,989
And sometimes nothing was copied at all.

57
00:03:01,559 --> 00:03:03,069
There are only so many notes.

58
00:03:04,019 --> 00:03:05,989
Only so many chord progressions.

59
00:03:06,419 --> 00:03:07,829
Only so many rhythms.

60
00:03:08,739 --> 00:03:13,389
With millions of songs, resemblance
is not suspicious. It is arithmetic.

61
00:03:14,473 --> 00:03:18,453
In two thousand and eighteen an
American appeal court upheld a verdict

62
00:03:18,533 --> 00:03:22,643
of over five million dollars against
the writers of Blurred Lines.

63
00:03:23,507 --> 00:03:28,397
The dissent pointed out that the two songs
differed in melody, harmony, and rhythm.

64
00:03:29,387 --> 00:03:31,187
It warned that the
majority was effectively

65
00:03:31,267 --> 00:03:33,957
letting someone copyright a musical style.

66
00:03:34,763 --> 00:03:38,253
So the law cannot simply ask
whether two things sound alike.

67
00:03:39,123 --> 00:03:41,153
It has to ask what was actually copied.

68
00:03:42,071 --> 00:03:43,441
A melody? A rhythm?

69
00:03:43,971 --> 00:03:45,761
A protected musical expression?

70
00:03:46,031 --> 00:03:48,361
Or something that belongs to everybody?

71
00:03:50,781 --> 00:03:54,111
Software has the same
problem, only far more openly.

72
00:03:55,061 --> 00:03:57,491
Modern software is built
out of other software:

73
00:03:57,741 --> 00:04:01,211
libraries, frameworks,
operating systems, protocols.

74
00:04:02,058 --> 00:04:06,348
Now take the harder case. You
buy a device. You take it apart.

75
00:04:07,198 --> 00:04:09,128
You work out how it does what it does.

76
00:04:09,798 --> 00:04:11,268
And you build your own version.

77
00:04:12,144 --> 00:04:15,654
You did not copy the product.
You extracted the knowledge.

78
00:04:16,573 --> 00:04:19,643
In nineteen ninety-two
an American court agreed.

79
00:04:20,393 --> 00:04:25,243
Copying a games console's code in order
to understand it counted as fair use.

80
00:04:26,015 --> 00:04:30,005
Not because copying is harmless, but
because there was no other way in.

81
00:04:30,697 --> 00:04:35,447
In two thousand and twenty-one the
American Supreme Court went further still.

82
00:04:36,357 --> 00:04:37,905
Google had reused eleven thousand five

83
00:04:37,985 --> 00:04:40,947
hundred lines of somebody
else's interface.

84
00:04:43,569 --> 00:04:44,769
Four tenths of one per
cent of the whole system.

85
00:04:45,505 --> 00:04:48,945
The court called it fair use,
six votes to two, because it let

86
00:04:49,025 --> 00:04:52,075
programmers carry what they
already knew into somewhere new.

87
00:04:54,595 --> 00:04:56,625
Which takes us somewhere uncomfortable.

88
00:04:57,677 --> 00:05:00,520
Think of a photograph
from nineteen thirty.

89
00:05:00,817 --> 00:05:02,287
The photographer may be dead.

90
00:05:02,977 --> 00:05:05,407
Their family may not know
the photograph exists.

91
00:05:06,257 --> 00:05:07,707
The publisher may be gone.

92
00:05:08,489 --> 00:05:10,259
You want it for a documentary.

93
00:05:11,149 --> 00:05:14,879
You cannot ask permission, because
you cannot find anybody to ask.

94
00:05:15,669 --> 00:05:19,219
That is an orphan work.
It exists. It has value.

95
00:05:19,969 --> 00:05:20,969
Nobody is earning from it.

96
00:05:21,049 --> 00:05:22,859
And using it is still a risk.

97
00:05:23,513 --> 00:05:26,543
Europe tried to fix this in twenty twelve.

98
00:05:26,853 --> 00:05:29,193
You may use an orphan
work, provided you first

99
00:05:29,273 --> 00:05:32,023
carry out what the law
calls a diligent search.

100
00:05:32,827 --> 00:05:35,847
So the price of using an
abandoned photograph is the

101
00:05:35,927 --> 00:05:38,377
price of proving that you
went looking for its owner.

102
00:05:39,131 --> 00:05:40,681
And so the work stays unused.

103
00:05:41,131 --> 00:05:44,021
Not because anyone wants
to keep it from you.

104
00:05:44,391 --> 00:05:47,441
But because nobody knows
who is allowed to say yes.

105
00:05:50,051 --> 00:05:52,161
So should we simply abolish copyright?

106
00:05:53,177 --> 00:05:54,377
Probably not.

107
00:05:55,857 --> 00:05:58,777
Imagine a film that costs
a hundred million dollars.

108
00:05:58,857 --> 00:06:00,877
Thousands of people.

109
00:06:00,957 --> 00:06:02,667
Years of work.

110
00:06:03,497 --> 00:06:07,427
And on the day it opens, somebody sells
a perfect copy for almost nothing.

111
00:06:08,161 --> 00:06:11,411
That destroys the reason to
make the expensive thing at all.

112
00:06:12,140 --> 00:06:15,030
"Everything should be free"
does not solve the problem.

113
00:06:15,820 --> 00:06:17,710
It just moves it somewhere else.

114
00:06:18,531 --> 00:06:22,941
So the question is not copyright
or no copyright. It is how much.

115
00:06:25,791 --> 00:06:27,901
And some of the answers already exist.

116
00:06:28,971 --> 00:06:31,611
If you want to record your
own version of somebody else's

117
00:06:31,691 --> 00:06:35,121
song in the United States, you
do not need their permission.

118
00:06:36,065 --> 00:06:38,495
You file a notice. You pay a fixed rate.

119
00:06:39,025 --> 00:06:40,735
That is the entire process.

120
00:06:41,646 --> 00:06:43,616
That system was written
in nineteen oh nine.

121
00:06:45,155 --> 00:06:47,985
Compulsory licensing is
not a thought experiment.

122
00:06:48,935 --> 00:06:51,485
It is older than almost
everybody using it.

123
00:06:53,525 --> 00:06:59,355
The same idea could cover sampling. You
used two seconds? Report it. Pay the rate.

124
00:06:59,485 --> 00:07:00,543
Done.

125
00:07:00,623 --> 00:07:01,943
No months of negotiation.

126
00:07:02,023 --> 00:07:06,913
No hunting for whoever owns three seconds
of a recording from nineteen seventy-four.

127
00:07:08,785 --> 00:07:11,125
And the law could take
transformation more seriously.

128
00:07:11,205 --> 00:07:16,695
Pirating a film is not the same thing
as making a documentary about it.

129
00:07:17,373 --> 00:07:22,163
Uploading somebody's song is not the
same thing as sampling two seconds of it.

130
00:07:22,803 --> 00:07:25,423
Copying a rival's code
is not the same thing

131
00:07:25,503 --> 00:07:28,333
as building something
that works alongside it.

132
00:07:29,579 --> 00:07:32,569
If you want the next one,
there is a subscribe button.

133
00:07:33,365 --> 00:07:35,875
And then, of course,
we built generative AI.

134
00:07:36,853 --> 00:07:39,103
Can a model learn from copyrighted books?

135
00:07:39,673 --> 00:07:41,263
From copyrighted music?

136
00:07:41,893 --> 00:07:44,423
Can it imitate somebody's
particular style?

137
00:07:44,773 --> 00:07:47,303
And if it can, who owns what comes out?

138
00:07:48,091 --> 00:07:52,041
In twenty twenty-five two American
courts answered part of it.

139
00:07:52,571 --> 00:07:54,891
Training a model on copyrighted books was

140
00:07:54,971 --> 00:07:57,501
transformative enough
to count as fair use.

141
00:07:58,259 --> 00:08:00,299
But one of those same rulings found that

142
00:08:00,379 --> 00:08:03,129
keeping a library of
pirated books was not.

143
00:08:03,923 --> 00:08:07,653
Which is exactly the distinction this
whole argument has been circling.

144
00:08:08,383 --> 00:08:09,973
Not, did you use it.

145
00:08:10,603 --> 00:08:12,173
But, what did you do with it.

146
00:08:12,919 --> 00:08:15,109
AI did not create that question.

147
00:08:15,839 --> 00:08:18,209
It just made it impossible
to keep avoiding.

148
00:08:21,059 --> 00:08:23,269
Because there is no free option here.

149
00:08:23,979 --> 00:08:26,669
Strong protection gives
people a reason to invest.

150
00:08:27,519 --> 00:08:30,229
It also makes existing
culture harder to reuse.

151
00:08:31,164 --> 00:08:33,994
Weak protection makes
building on the past easier.

152
00:08:34,824 --> 00:08:37,754
It also makes expensive
work harder to pay for.

153
00:08:38,515 --> 00:08:40,595
Which is why the goal
was never to maximise

154
00:08:40,675 --> 00:08:43,645
protection, and never
to minimise it either.

155
00:08:44,595 --> 00:08:46,875
The goal was to maximise
how much gets made.

156
00:08:46,955 --> 00:08:49,265
And those are not the same thing.

157
00:08:50,627 --> 00:08:54,177
So perhaps the real question is
not how we stop people copying.

158
00:08:54,627 --> 00:08:57,977
It is how we stop free-riding
without stopping building.

159
00:08:58,725 --> 00:09:00,425
And the most important
thing about copyright

160
00:09:00,505 --> 00:09:03,295
may not be what it hands today's creators.

161
00:09:04,225 --> 00:09:06,295
It is what it leaves for tomorrow's.

162
00:09:06,887 --> 00:09:09,237
Because if we guard the
past too carefully...

163
00:09:10,697 --> 00:09:14,117
we end up with a system so good
at protecting what has already

164
00:09:14,197 --> 00:09:17,607
been made that making the next
thing is unnecessarily hard.

165
00:09:18,345 --> 00:09:21,315
And that would be a very strange
way to encourage progress.
